<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Radio degradation without moving anything, driven by INET's
    ScenarioManager (see [Config UplinkRadioImpairment] in omnetpp_uplink.ini).

    ue0_walkaway.xml does the same thing the way stock Simu5G forces you to:
    channel quality is a function of geometry, so the only lever is to walk the
    UE away from the gNodeB and wait for the distance to build up. Every direct
    knob of the channel model is cached into a C++ member during initialize()
    (LteRealisticChannelModel.cc:47-92), so <set-param> on it changes the
    parameter and nothing ever reads it again.

    TunableNrChannelModel is that missing lever: the same NrChannelModel, plus
    two parameters that are re-read on every call and annotated @mutable.
    Nothing moves, and the change lands on the next transmission.

    The module addressed here is the gNodeB's channel model because this is an
    uplink scenario. The SINR of an uplink transmission is computed by its
    receiver, and so is the CQI the gNodeB feeds back to the UE. Note the path:
    channelModel[] hangs off the NIC, not off the phy (LteNicBase.ned:109).

    What each step should do to the traffic:

      t=2s   sinrOffset 0dB to 12dB
             Enough to cost several CQI steps. The scheduler answers with a
             more robust MCS and more resource blocks per packet, and the odd
             transmission now fails the BLER draw and is retransmitted by HARQ.
             End-to-end latency and jitter rise; loss stays near zero.

      t=3.5s extraPer 0 to 0.3
             Loss the scheduler cannot adapt to, since it never reaches the
             CQI: three transmission attempts in ten are simply discarded.
             HARQ absorbs most of it, at the price of more latency again.

      t=4.5s both back to nominal
             Shows the link recovering, which the walk-away scenario cannot do
             without walking the UE back.
-->
<scenario>
    <at t="2s">
        <set-param module="gnb.cellularNic.channelModel[0]"
                   par="sinrOffset" value="12dB"/>
    </at>
    <at t="3.5s">
        <set-param module="gnb.cellularNic.channelModel[0]"
                   par="extraPer" value="0.3"/>
    </at>
    <at t="4.5s">
        <set-param module="gnb.cellularNic.channelModel[0]"
                   par="sinrOffset" value="0dB"/>
        <set-param module="gnb.cellularNic.channelModel[0]"
                   par="extraPer" value="0"/>
    </at>
</scenario>
